UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 23, Lesson 1: Seasons', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length. A useful Stage 2 model links seasons to Earth’s yearly path and tilt (keep language age-right: tilt means parts of Earth lean toward/away from Sun across the year).","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort typical NSW: beach weather and long daylight; frost mornings and early dark — which seasons?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSummer-like; winter-like.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up; tilt/day length ideas matter more in the school model.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a seasons poster for your place: typical weather, day length idea and two living-thing changes per season. State Australia/NSW context.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length. A useful Stage 2 model links seasons to Earth’s yearly path and tilt (keep language age-right: tilt means parts of Earth lean toward/away from Sun across the year).","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a seasons poster for your place: typical weather, day length idea and two living-thing changes per season. State Australia/NSW context. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about season linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up;. Correction: Summer-like; winter-like. with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up; tilt/day length ideas matter more in the school model. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses season. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about season, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about season. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for season. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for season. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Summer-like; winter-like"],"question":"Sort typical NSW: beach weather and long daylight; frost mornings and early dark — which seasons?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for season; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of tilt and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up","Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length","Summer-like; winter-like"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with season?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named tilt and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up","Name tilt and explain why it matters only, without linking it to season","Apply season to new material and name the tilt and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies season with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up"],"question":"New examples: steam from a kettle, a germinating bean, a plastic dinosaur, a granite pebble. Which claim about season is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence","Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up","A named observation plus how it supports the claim"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about season?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about season with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Summer-like; winter-like","Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer"],"question":"Using this science idea — Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length. — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about season?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about season should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true","Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up"],"question":"Which prediction about season can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about season. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses season. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about season linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for season. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about season, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up;. Correction: Summer-like; winter-like. with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w23-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise season.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Earth and Space","success_criteria":["I can use season with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify tilt and explain why it matters.","I can practise year independently on a new prompt.","I can notice pattern needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about season with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn Australia, summer tends to be warmer with longer days; winter cooler with shorter days. Seasons reverse between northern and southern hemispheres.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort typical NSW: beach weather and long daylight; frost mornings and early dark — which seasons?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSummer-like; winter-like.","explain":"Seasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length. A useful Stage 2 model links seasons to Earth’s yearly path and tilt (keep language age-right: tilt means parts of Earth lean toward/away from Sun across the year).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up; tilt/day length ideas matter more in the school model. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Seasons in context.","caption":"Tilt/day length matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Season</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">yearly pattern</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Australia</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">summer longer days</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: In Australia, summer tends to be warmer with longer days; winter cooler with shorter days. Seasons reverse between north Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: In Australia, summer tends to be warmer with longer days; winter cooler with shorter days. Seasons reverse between north Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about season with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn Australia, summer tends to be warmer with longer days; winter cooler with shorter days. Seasons reverse between northern and southern hemispheres. Look at each observation, variable or design choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Season contrasts.","caption":"NSW examples on poster.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Summer</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">warmer longer light</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Winter</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">cooler shorter light</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about season with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up; tilt/day length ideas matter more in the school model. Here is a more productive model: In Australia, summer tends to be warmer with longer days; winter cooler with shorter days. Seasons reverse between northern and southern hemispheres.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSummer-like; winter-like.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking Earth is closer to the Sun in summer (only) is a common mix-up; tilt/day length ideas matter more in the school model. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about season with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSeasons are yearly patterns of weather and day length. A useful Stage 2 model links seasons to Earth’s yearly path and tilt (keep language age-right: tilt means parts of Earth lean toward/away from Sun across the year). Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvestigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 23, Lesson 1: Seasons","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Earth, Sun and Moon","week":23,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nIn Australia, summer tends to be warmer with longer days; winter cooler with shorter days. Seasons reverse between northern and southern hemispheres.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Seasons in context.","caption":"Tilt/day length matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" heig